    "q-94088": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94088",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z<-0.36).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3264\\,$ | $\\,0.3228\\,$ | $\\,0.3192\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ | $\\,0.3594\\,$ | $\\,0.3557\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ | $\\,0.3974\\,$ | $\\,0.3936\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "If $Z∼N(0,1),$ then by the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nAlso, since $Z$ is continuous, \n\n$$\nP(Z<-0.36)=P(Z\\le -0.36)=Φ(-0.36).\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14254773/assets/06ab72c4b54e672a81892dbb8e7193dec7dc6d75b9b13bfcd1abe7286211a4a5.png)\n\nNow, we determine the value of $Φ(-0.36)$ from the table:\n\n- We first select the row that corresponds to $-0.3$ and the column that corresponds to $0.06.$\n\n- Then, we take the value that lies at the intersection:\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3264\\,$ | $\\,0.3228\\,$ | $\\,0.3192\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ | $\\,0.3594\\,$ | $\\,0.3557\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ | $\\,0.3974\\,$ | $\\,0.3936\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, according to the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(Z\\le -0.36)=Φ(-0.36)=0.3594.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
